  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cloudy finish to the weekend, with the potential\nfor frost and freezing conditions west of the Blue Ridge by late\nMonday.\n\nA cold air damming (CAD) wedge remains in place across the Mid-\nAtlantic region. The persistent low-level north to northeasterly\nwinds have maintained a stratus deck with some areas of drizzle.\nHowever, any of the more concentrated rainfall remains well to\nthe south around the Virginia Tidewater region. While the mid-\nlevel water vapor channel and earlier IAD sounding showed a dry\nmid-level layer, immense moisture remains trapped in the stable\nlower atmosphere. For the remainder of the day, expect\ntemperatures to hold steady in the 60s. With some cloud breaks\nacross the Allegheny Front, spotty low 70s are possible across\nthe mountain valleys. Overnight conditions stay cloudy,\naccompanied by some patchy fog development.\n\nAny residual cloud cover should erode as a cold front sweeps \nthrough the area early Monday morning. This yields a mostly \nsunny day with temperatures warming back into the upper 60s to \nmid 70s, with 60s across the mountains. Northwesterly winds pick\nup behind the frontal system with afternoon gusts to around 20 \nto 25 mph. A strong area of Canadian high pressure pushes in \nfrom the Midwest on Monday before settling over the local area \nby the overnight period. This sets the stages for the first \nwidespread frost event, mainly west of the Blue Ridge. The \nAllegheny ridgetops could even near the freezing mark. For east \nof the Blue Ridge, forecast lows will be in the 40s.\n\nHigh pressure maintains its influence over the area on Tuesday \nwhich brings cool temperatures, lighter winds, and sunny skies.\nMost can expect highs in the 60s, with mid/upper 50s across\nmountain locales. Tuesday night also looks chilly, but forecast\ntemperatures have come up a tad given light southerly flow\naround the departing high pressure center. At this point\ntemperatures would be just mild enough to avoid another frost\nevent. For most, the forecast lows are in the low/mid 40s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions and warmer temperatures are\nforecast for Wednesday through Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in control locally through much of the \nupcoming week. Dry conditions are forecast for Wednesday through \nSaturday, along with warmer temperatures. Highs each day will \ngenerally be in the 70s, with lows in the upper 40s to lower 50s.\n\nThere are signs that the stretch of dry weather will come to an end \non Sunday as an area of low pressure tracks northward out of the \nGulf toward the Ohio Valley. If this occurs, rain will likely move \ninto the area from the south. However, there's still a lot of \nvariability in model guidance with respect to what will occur with \nthis system, and if/when we'll get rain.",
